{"name":"Emerald Coast","hex":"#009185","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"emerald","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various green gemstones, especially a green transparent form of beryl, highly valued as a precious stone.","Emerald green, a colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rich green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/emerald"},{"term":"coast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The edge of the land where it meets an ocean, sea, gulf, bay, or large lake. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The side or edge of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To glide along without adding energy; to allow a vehicle to continue moving forward after disengaging the engine or ceasing to apply motive power.","To sail along a coast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Emerald Coast","blocks":[{"text":"The Emerald Coast is an unofficial name for the coastal area in the US state of Florida on the Gulf of Mexico that stretches about 100 miles (160 km) through five counties—Escambia, Santa Rosa, Okaloosa, Walton, and Bay—which include Pensacola Beach, Navarre Beach, Fort Walton Beach, Destin, and Panama City Beach. Some southern Alabama communities on the coast of Baldwin County, such as Gulf Shores, Orange Beach, and Fort Morgan, use the term as well."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Emerald_Coast_Florida.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerald_Coast"},{"title":"Emerald","blocks":[{"text":"Emerald is a gemstone and a variety of the mineral beryl (Be3Al2(SiO3)6) colored green by trace amounts of chromium or sometimes vanadium. Beryl has a hardness of 7.5–8 on the Mohs scale. Most emeralds have many inclusions, so their toughness is classified as poor. Emerald is a cyclosilicate. It occurs mainly in association with quartz, muscovite, albite, mica schist, Pyrite schorl, microcline, fluorite, smoky quartz and elbaite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/B%C3%A9ryl_var._%C3%A9meraude_sur_gangue_%28Muzo_Mine_Boyaca_-_Colombie%29_2.jpg/330px-B%C3%A9ryl_var._%C3%A9meraude_sur_gangue_%28Muzo_Mine_Boyaca_-_Colombie%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerald"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg/500px-Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg/1280px-Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg/500px-Emerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emerald-green waters at St. Andrews State Park, near the eastern edge of the Emerald Coast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmerald_Coast_Waters_from_St_Andrews_State_Park.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/PensacolaBeachFlorida.JPG/500px-PensacolaBeachFlorida.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/PensacolaBeachFlorida.JPG/500px-PensacolaBeachFlorida.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pensacola Beach, the western part of the Emerald Coast\n","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APensacolaBeachFlorida.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg/500px-Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg/1280px-Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg/500px-Beach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beach in Destin, at the center of the Coast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeach_in_Destin%2C_Florida.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.w